---------------------Topic 1: Executor Framework —------------------

----> Why do we need it?

Normally, we can create threads like this:

Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Task 1");
});

Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
    System.out.println("Task 2");
});

t1.start();
t2.start();

This works, but imagine your backend receives 1,000 tasks.

Creating 1,000 threads is not a good approach:

1000 Tasks
   ↓
1000 Threads ❌
   ↓
High memory + overhead

The Executor Framework solves this by managing threads for us.

1000 Tasks
    ↓
ExecutorService
    ↓
Thread Pool
 ┌────┬────┬────┐
 T1   T2   T3   T4
 └────┴────┴────┘

Instead of creating a new thread for every task, we create a pool of reusable threads.